1
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:12,190
All right, next up is the final chapter on assembly instructions.

2
00:00:12,870 --> 00:00:17,630
This part mainly introduces the concept of mixing assembly and C programming,

3
00:00:17,910 --> 00:00:25,890
because it will come up in our code later, so we'll talk about it here.

4
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:31,490
So mixing assembly and C really falls into two main categories,

5
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:34,070
one is calling C functions from assembly

6
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:35,210
that's one

7
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:36,430
and the other way around

8
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:37,310
which is in C

9
00:00:37,310 --> 00:00:39,930
we can also embed calls to some

10
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:41,630
or write some assembly code

11
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:44,650
These are techniques you'll often use in low-level development.

12
00:00:44,650 --> 00:00:46,210
So let me walk you through them here.

13
00:00:46,210 --> 00:00:47,910
Let's start with the first part,

14
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:49,370
which is assembly calling C.

15
00:00:49,370 --> 00:00:51,390
This part is actually pretty simple,

16
00:00:51,390 --> 00:00:54,230
because in the exercise we just did

17
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:55,690
this concept already showed up

18
00:00:55,690 --> 00:00:56,250
right?

19
00:00:56,250 --> 00:01:02,470
We've already touched on some of these related things.

20
00:01:02,470 --> 00:01:03,850
Assembly calling C

21
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:06,390
Now let's take

22
00:01:06,390 --> 00:01:07,730
a look at this together.

23
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:09,210
That is to say, C

24
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:11,090
doesn't really have anything special about it.

25
00:01:11,090 --> 00:01:13,530
C is, at the end of the day, just a high-level language.

26
00:01:13,530 --> 00:01:14,870
After it's compiled,

27
00:01:14,870 --> 00:01:17,950
it's really just assembly code and machine instructions.

28
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:21,410
So as long as we follow

29
00:01:21,410 --> 00:01:24,210
the function calling process we just talked about,

30
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:24,830
the calling convention,

31
00:01:24,830 --> 00:01:26,630
we can actually write

32
00:01:26,630 --> 00:01:29,670
correct

33
00:01:29,670 --> 00:01:32,950
assembly-calling-C handling like this.

34
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:35,290
Now here,

35
00:01:35,290 --> 00:01:37,030
the main thing is to follow the ABI,

36
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:37,990
which is what we just mentioned.

37
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:39,070
That is to say, the ABI,

38
00:01:39,070 --> 00:01:42,670
which stands for Abstract Binary Interface,

39
00:01:42,670 --> 00:01:45,950
actually involves what we just talked about,

40
00:01:45,950 --> 00:01:47,470
for example, the size of data types.

41
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:50,950
That is to say, whether it's assembly or C,

42
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:52,290
you actually deal with data types,

43
00:01:52,290 --> 00:01:53,730
for example, how many bytes an int takes up,

44
00:01:53,730 --> 00:01:57,450
how many bytes a char takes up,

45
00:01:57,450 --> 00:02:00,930
how many characters a character takes up—

46
00:02:00,930 --> 00:02:01,650
how many bytes it takes up,

47
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:02,590
concepts like that.

48
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:04,370
Then there are also some memory

49
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:05,330
alignment issues,

50
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:06,230
four-byte alignment,

51
00:02:06,230 --> 00:02:07,510
and all that kind of thing.

52
00:02:07,510 --> 00:02:10,290
Actually, as long as it's cross-language,

53
00:02:10,290 --> 00:02:11,830
underneath it's all the same—

54
00:02:11,830 --> 00:02:12,610
you have to deal with all of it.

55
00:02:12,610 --> 00:02:14,070
Then there's also function calls,

56
00:02:14,070 --> 00:02:15,550
which we just covered.

57
00:02:15,550 --> 00:02:16,330
System calls—

58
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:17,750
this might involve,

59
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:20,170
mainly, the applications or user-mode programs,

60
00:02:20,170 --> 00:02:23,450
and it's a concept about the interface between them and the operating system.

61
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:25,870
Let's not go into that here.

62
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:27,710
It's not really related to what we're discussing.

63
00:02:27,710 --> 00:02:29,210
Then there's another thing,

64
00:02:29,210 --> 00:02:30,290
when making a function call,

65
00:02:30,290 --> 00:02:31,330
there are certain conventions,

66
00:02:31,330 --> 00:02:32,130
for example, the registers,

67
00:02:32,130 --> 00:02:34,610
we use a0 and a7 to pass parameters.

68
00:02:34,610 --> 00:02:35,790
The return values go in a0 and a1.

69
00:02:35,790 --> 00:02:37,110
These are all things we covered earlier.

70
00:02:37,110 --> 00:02:38,410
They become part of the specification.

71
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:41,570
So for a specific example,

72
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:42,650
let's take a quick look at one.

73
00:02:42,650 --> 00:02:43,510
Very simple.

74
00:02:43,510 --> 00:02:44,770
This example,

75
00:02:44,770 --> 00:02:46,230
let's take a look at it.

76
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:47,770
It's ASM to C.

77
00:02:47,770 --> 00:02:49,410
ASM stands for

78
00:02:49,410 --> 00:02:56,820
ASM stands for

79
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:57,800
the abbreviation of 'assembly'.

80
00:02:57,800 --> 00:02:58,580
And 'to' m

81
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:00,000
And this 2 here is "to"

82
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,080
In English, it's just "to"

83
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:03,520
As in from S-mode to C

84
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:04,020
Right?

85
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:04,680
That's what it means.

86
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:07,780
Just wanted to tell you all

87
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:09,100
a bit about the naming here,

88
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:09,700
why it's called this.

89
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:12,420
So over here,

90
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:13,540
we can see

91
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:14,460
that down here,

92
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:17,480
we actually have an assembly instruction,

93
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:19,920
the test.s file,

94
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:20,760
and right here,

95
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:21,680
we have a test.c.

96
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:23,120
So this is essentially mixed programming.

97
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:24,560
That is, what we have here is

98
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:25,220
actually, we're...

99
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:28,520
we start executing from the assembly in test.c first,

100
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:29,260
and during execution,

101
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:31,920
it will jump into this test.c

102
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:33,300
So how does it jump?

103
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:34,300
It's very simple.

104
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:34,960
Let's take a look.

105
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:38,060
First, let's look at this test.c.

106
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:42,080
This function isn't much different from what we talked about earlier.

107
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:43,420
The main things to understand here are:

108
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:44,040
One is that

109
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:46,540
we first need to prepare a stack area.

110
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:48,460
There's nothing much to say about this.

111
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:51,460
Whether it's C or assembly, we're all writing functions.

112
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:52,800
And functions all need to use the stack.

113
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:54,500
So we all need to prepare a stack

114
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:55,480
area,

115
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:58,100
and we need to take this stack

116
00:03:58,100 --> 00:03:59,060
and initialize it.

117
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:00,900
We've covered this before.

118
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:02,360
Then next,

119
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:03,600
that is, we need to

120
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:05,460
go from this start function

121
00:04:05,460 --> 00:04:06,380
Start executing.

122
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:07,580
In the course of the execution,

123
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:09,660
we'll call a function foo.

124
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:11,080
This function foo

125
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:12,460
is just a function written in C.

126
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:14,400
Now, as we mentioned earlier,

127
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:16,780
a function name is actually the address of that function.

128
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:17,040
Right?

129
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:18,700
When we learned C,

130
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:20,540
we also talked about the concept of function pointers.

131
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:21,820
The function name is the function's address.

132
00:04:21,820 --> 00:04:23,740
So when we're in assembly

133
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:25,280
and want to call a C function,

134
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:27,680
all we actually need to do is take that C

135
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:28,640
function's name

136
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:29,600
and write it right here.

137
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:31,300
Right?

138
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:32,920
It's really just a label.

139
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:33,620
Just a label.

140
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:35,540
So when we call

141
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:36,820
followed by a function name,

142
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:38,680
it will naturally jump to that function and execute it.

143
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:40,080
It's that simple.

144
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:41,500
So the only thing we need to do,

145
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:42,580
is that if we want to pass parameters,

146
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:43,920
then here,

147
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:44,840
we just need to

148
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:47,440
use a0 and a1,

149
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:49,000
the two standard conventions,

150
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:49,920
to pass the parameters, and that's it.

151
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:51,180
Everything before this is the same.

152
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:53,040
We pass 1 and 2 in.

153
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:54,180
Now this function,

154
00:04:54,180 --> 00:04:56,300
it takes two parameters,

155
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:58,040
a and b.

156
00:04:58,040 --> 00:04:59,180
So we pass the parameters,

157
00:04:59,180 --> 00:04:59,860
and then,

158
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:02,580
and then let's pay attention here,

159
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:03,060
at this point,

160
00:05:03,060 --> 00:05:05,300
we need to take this function foo,

161
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:07,500
We use the Global directive

162
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:08,480
to declare it.

163
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:09,740
Because this function

164
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:11,540
is not defined in this file,

165
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:12,280
you see?

166
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:13,420
This is basically

167
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:15,280
the same effect as extern in C.

168
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:17,200
In other words, we tell the compiler

169
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:18,640
that this function F0

170
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:19,640
is defined elsewhere,

171
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:21,280
that it's a global thing.

172
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:22,920
Then when it compiles,

173
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:23,800
it won't report errors again,

174
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:25,040
saying it can't find this function, and so on.

175
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:28,060
Because this function

176
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:30,060
is defined in test.c,

177
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:31,820
so we need to declare it here.

178
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:34,260
So basically, for the assembly part,

179
00:05:34,260 --> 00:05:35,660
that covers all the content involved.

180
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:38,040
And as for the C part, there's really no difference in how it's written.

181
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:41,640
Just write it the way it's supposed to be written.

182
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:43,180
As for C, well, I think...

183
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:45,720
With this function we have now,

184
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:46,940
we don't need to explain it much more.

185
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:48,100
Now it's just about saying,

186
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:50,880
when you all look at this function now,

187
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:52,840
aren't you already thinking in your heads

188
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:55,280
about the assembly language behind it,

189
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:56,400
what kind of concept that is?

190
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:59,420
Once you've studied this,

191
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:01,140
the way you look at C functions

192
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:02,560
might have a certain...

193
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:05,300
what do you call it?

194
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:07,340
Do you call it a sublimation? Like, an elevation?

195
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:08,160
Or some other concept?

196
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:10,760
Meaning, the way you look at C now,

197
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:12,300
actually, if you can... behind it...

198
00:06:12,300 --> 00:06:13,300
the moment you see C,

199
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:15,140
you can picture the assembly behind it,

200
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:16,280
roughly what it looks like.

201
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:17,140
Well, congratulations.

202
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:18,220
That is to say, I think this course,

203
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:19,700
at least you haven't learned it for nothing.

204
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:22,340
Now, when you look at this C code,

205
00:06:22,340 --> 00:06:23,800
this thing is actually just a label.

206
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:26,240
Now, its two parameters,

207
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:28,520
well, we can all imagine,

208
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:31,500
when this code is handed over to the compiler to compile,

209
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:32,900
when it generates the code for you,

210
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:36,260
it will definitely help you put A0 and A1,

211
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:38,320
which actually correspond to A and B.

212
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,460
At the same time,

213
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:41,940
the return value C here,

