00:08:46,270 --> 00:08:48,290
Now for the second one,

285
00:08:48,290 --> 00:08:49,670
what we want to tell you is

286
00:08:49,670 --> 00:08:50,710
in C language,

287
00:08:50,710 --> 00:08:52,830
if we want to embed some

288
00:08:52,830 --> 00:08:55,710
assembly, how do we do it?

289
00:08:55,710 --> 00:08:57,510
Just understand this.

290
00:08:57,510 --> 00:08:59,930
First of all,

291
00:08:59,930 --> 00:09:01,730
if you want to embed assembly in C,

292
00:09:01,730 --> 00:09:04,310
you need to write this kind of keyword,

293
00:09:04,310 --> 00:09:06,630
that is, a keyword starting with ASM.

294
00:09:06,630 --> 00:09:08,510
The syntax is like this.

295
00:09:08,510 --> 00:09:09,990
ASM is a keyword,

296
00:09:09,990 --> 00:09:11,730
and the square brackets after it mean

297
00:09:11,730 --> 00:09:14,350
this keyword is optional.

298
00:09:14,350 --> 00:09:16,210
Then it's wrapped in parentheses,

299
00:09:16,210 --> 00:09:18,830
and inside are the assembly instructions,

300
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:20,570
while outside it's C language code.

301
00:09:20,570 --> 00:09:22,450
Out here, everything is C language instructions.

302
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:24,850
In here, in the middle, we embed

303
00:09:24,850 --> 00:09:26,250
a piece of assembly like this.

304
00:09:26,250 --> 00:09:26,790
That's how you write it.

305
00:09:26,790 --> 00:09:28,190
Now, at the front,

306
00:09:28,190 --> 00:09:28,810
there are keywords.

307
00:09:28,810 --> 00:09:29,750
And then, volatile,

308
00:09:29,750 --> 00:09:30,450
it means that,

309
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:31,230
if you add this,

310
00:09:31,230 --> 00:09:32,870
you are telling the compiler,

311
00:09:32,870 --> 00:09:34,790
these assembly instructions inside here,

312
00:09:34,790 --> 00:09:37,290
you don't want it to optimize them.

313
00:09:37,290 --> 00:09:38,610
If you do want optimization,

314
00:09:38,610 --> 00:09:40,410
because the compiler might try

315
00:09:40,410 --> 00:09:41,890
to optimize your instructions,

316
00:09:41,890 --> 00:09:44,330
so if you don't want optimization,

317
00:09:44,330 --> 00:09:45,530
just add a volatile.

318
00:09:45,530 --> 00:09:47,810
If you don't care,

319
00:09:47,810 --> 00:09:48,290
just leave it out.

320
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:49,770
So this part is optional.

321
00:09:49,770 --> 00:09:49,950
This

322
00:09:49,950 --> 00:09:52,650
sometimes you'll see

323
00:09:52,650 --> 00:09:53,870
in front of this there are some additional

324
00:09:53,870 --> 00:09:55,390
double slash things like this

325
00:09:55,390 --> 00:09:56,750
which actually have the same effect

326
00:09:56,750 --> 00:09:57,970
I won't go into that in detail

327
00:09:57,970 --> 00:09:59,150
and then

328
00:09:59,150 --> 00:09:59,650
this is

329
00:09:59,650 --> 00:10:01,930
this is 'asm', which is the keyword

330
00:10:01,930 --> 00:10:02,950
and then

331
00:10:02,950 --> 00:10:04,710
inside it there are four main parts

332
00:10:04,710 --> 00:10:06,570
the first part is wrapped in quotes

333
00:10:06,570 --> 00:10:07,710
enclosed

334
00:10:07,710 --> 00:10:09,690
and this part is the core of it all

335
00:10:09,690 --> 00:10:10,990
this is where the assembly instructions are written

336
00:10:10,990 --> 00:10:12,870
now, for the assembly instructions,

337
00:10:12,870 --> 00:10:14,150
they are wrapped in double quotes here

338
00:10:14,150 --> 00:10:15,930
and if you need multiple instructions,

339
00:10:15,930 --> 00:10:18,630
you separate them with a semicolon or backslash n

340
00:10:18,630 --> 00:10:19,670
which represents a newline

341
00:10:19,670 --> 00:10:21,170
Just split it apart.

342
00:10:21,170 --> 00:10:22,490
This is its syntax.

343
00:10:22,490 --> 00:10:25,130
Then these three items below

344
00:10:25,130 --> 00:10:26,090
are all optional.

345
00:10:26,090 --> 00:10:26,790
Note carefully,

346
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:27,610
they are just

347
00:10:27,610 --> 00:10:29,050
these three items,

348
00:10:29,050 --> 00:10:30,970
they're not actually assembly instructions.

349
00:10:30,970 --> 00:10:32,970
They're really a kind of

350
00:10:32,970 --> 00:10:34,310
they're really a kind of directive statement,

351
00:10:34,310 --> 00:10:37,450
telling the assembler,

352
00:10:37,450 --> 00:10:39,010
for this assembly instruction in front of you,

353
00:10:39,010 --> 00:10:41,470
there are some special annotations.

354
00:10:41,470 --> 00:10:43,110
You can actually understand it that way.

355
00:10:43,110 --> 00:10:45,130
So what is it annotating?

356
00:10:45,130 --> 00:10:46,530
It's actually annotating

357
00:10:46,530 --> 00:10:48,150
in your assembler,

358
00:10:48,150 --> 00:10:49,490
for this assembly instruction,

359
00:10:49,490 --> 00:10:51,490
what output operands and input operands

360
00:10:51,490 --> 00:10:52,330
there are.

361
00:10:52,330 --> 00:10:54,330
as well as what might affect this thing,

362
00:10:54,330 --> 00:10:56,150
let me briefly introduce each of them to you.

363
00:10:56,150 --> 00:10:58,090
Let me show you through an example.

364
00:10:58,090 --> 00:11:01,090
What's on the right is actually an example.

365
00:11:01,090 --> 00:11:02,770
This example implements a

366
00:11:02,770 --> 00:11:05,310
we write a for function in C,

367
00:11:05,310 --> 00:11:06,610
and inside this function,

368
00:11:06,610 --> 00:11:09,250
what it actually does

369
00:11:09,250 --> 00:11:11,190
is implement an A plus B equals C,

370
00:11:11,190 --> 00:11:12,070
an effect like that.

371
00:11:12,070 --> 00:11:14,370
That is to say, if I don't use assembly,

372
00:11:14,370 --> 00:11:16,450
if this part is not written in assembly,

373
00:11:16,450 --> 00:11:19,970
it's nothing more than implementing C equals A plus B.

374
00:11:19,970 --> 00:11:24,150
But suppose one day I suddenly feel that

375
00:11:24,150 --> 00:11:26,130
C plus A -- C's A plus B -- is not efficient enough,

376
00:11:26,130 --> 00:11:27,810
and I insist on writing it myself in assembly,

377
00:11:27,810 --> 00:11:29,850
then I can still rewrite and change it.

378
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:31,110
So how do we write it?

379
00:11:31,110 --> 00:11:33,750
Well, based on the description just now,

380
00:11:33,750 --> 00:11:34,650
you've already seen

381
00:11:34,650 --> 00:11:36,930
That is to say, this is a keyword that must be written

382
00:11:36,930 --> 00:11:37,990
Adding a volatile

383
00:11:37,990 --> 00:11:40,170
is basically telling our assembler that

384
00:11:40,170 --> 00:11:42,930
for this assembly instruction in here

385
00:11:42,930 --> 00:11:44,270
I don't want you to do any optimization

386
00:11:44,270 --> 00:11:46,450
Just honestly follow whatever I wrote

387
00:11:46,450 --> 00:11:48,030
and generate exactly that

388
00:11:48,030 --> 00:11:50,150
Then, next,

389
00:11:50,150 --> 00:11:51,110
this here

390
00:11:51,110 --> 00:11:52,410
corresponds to this,

391
00:11:52,410 --> 00:11:54,070
the output operands.

392
00:11:54,070 --> 00:11:55,370
This part specifies

393
00:11:55,370 --> 00:11:56,730
and then this corresponds to

394
00:11:56,730 --> 00:11:58,330
the description of the input operands.

395
00:11:58,330 --> 00:12:00,050
And the third part,

396
00:12:00,050 --> 00:12:00,790
I just didn't write it

397
00:12:00,790 --> 00:12:02,430
because there isn't really much

398
00:12:02,430 --> 00:12:03,530
that needs to be described there,

399
00:12:03,530 --> 00:12:04,690
since I only used

400
00:12:04,690 --> 00:12:05,990
two registers anyway.

401
00:12:05,990 --> 00:12:09,130
So, what do these output operands

402
00:12:09,130 --> 00:12:10,450
and input operands mean?

403
00:12:10,450 --> 00:12:12,430
Well, the so-called output operands

404
00:12:12,430 --> 00:12:14,450
are essentially registers that I might

405
00:12:14,450 --> 00:12:17,770
write to—

406
00:12:17,770 --> 00:12:20,730
registers whose values I might change.

407
00:12:20,730 --> 00:12:23,490
Those registers count as output operands.

408
00:12:23,490 --> 00:12:25,290
That's the concept.

409
00:12:25,290 --> 00:12:27,790
Conversely, the input

410
00:12:27,790 --> 00:12:29,090
concept is the opposite.

411
00:12:29,090 --> 00:12:32,710
Now, what exactly am I explaining here?

412
00:12:32,710 --> 00:12:33,590
Take a look at this.

413
00:12:33,590 --> 00:12:36,970
Because when I was writing this,

414
00:12:36,970 --> 00:12:40,790
first of all, I couldn't immediately—

415
00:12:40,790 --> 00:12:44,110
that is, I didn't want to absolutely specify

416
00:12:44,110 --> 00:12:45,710
exactly which register to use,

417
00:12:45,710 --> 00:12:49,270
because which register you actually use,

418
00:12:49,270 --> 00:12:53,610
in practice, we generally try to leave that to the compiler

419
00:12:53,610 --> 00:12:55,110
to decide for us.

420
00:12:55,110 --> 00:12:56,230
We just tell it

421
00:12:56,230 --> 00:12:57,610
Here we need a register

422
00:12:57,610 --> 00:12:58,990
This needs a register

423
00:12:58,990 --> 00:13:00,050
This needs a register

424
00:13:00,050 --> 00:13:01,750
Then we take this register

425
00:13:01,750 --> 00:13:02,450
and we give it a name

426
00:13:02,450 --> 00:13:02,990
See that?

427
00:13:02,990 --> 00:13:04,230
We call it sum

428
00:13:04,230 --> 00:13:05,750
We call them add1 and add2

429
00:13:05,750 --> 00:13:07,930
These are not actual register names

430
00:13:07,930 --> 00:13:08,370
Right?

431
00:13:08,370 --> 00:13:10,790
What they actually turn into

432
00:13:10,790 --> 00:13:13,590
is something the compiler generates for us in practice

433
00:13:13,590 --> 00:13:16,730
We don't explicitly force that here

434
00:13:16,730 --> 00:13:18,630
The only thing we have to tell it is this

435
00:13:18,630 --> 00:13:19,670
What we need to tell it is this

436
00:13:19,670 --> 00:13:23,130
Since what we want to implement is C equals A plus B

437
00:13:23,130 --> 00:13:27,370
we have to tell the compiler

438
00:13:27,370 --> 00:13:29,250
that you need to take this C

439
00:13:29,250 --> 00:13:32,690
and associate it with this register

440
00:13:32,690 --> 00:13:35,030
and you need to associate this register A with that

441
00:13:35,030 --> 00:13:37,470
You need to associate B with this thing

442
00:13:37,470 --> 00:13:39,490
Then we tell it through an add instruction

443
00:13:39,490 --> 00:13:42,470
Just use this assembly instruction to achieve the effect, and that's it

444
00:13:42,470 --> 00:13:44,310
So how do we tell it

445
00:13:44,310 --> 00:13:45,990
About this correspondence between them?

446
00:13:45,990 --> 00:13:48,550
That is done through these two lines here

447
00:13:48,550 --> 00:13:50,750
Because as we said earlier

448
00:13:50,750 --> 00:13:53,290
This one specifies the output

449
00:13:53,290 --> 00:13:57,210
That is, which register I'm going to write to

450
00:13:57,210 --> 00:13:58,570
So on my side here

451
00:13:58,570 --> 00:14:00,590
I tell it that the sum register

452
00:14:00,590 --> 00:14:02,470
corresponds to this c of ours

453
00:14:02,470 --> 00:14:03,710
Because in the end we have to return this c

454
00:14:03,710 --> 00:14:05,070
We have to pass it back out, right

455
00:14:05,070 --> 00:14:06,730
So we tell it c

456
00:14:06,730 --> 00:14:08,370
Then what about add1?

457
00:14:08,370 --> 00:14:09,310
And what about add2?

458
00:14:09,310 --> 00:14:12,190
They correspond to a and b

459
00:14:12,190 --> 00:14:16,910
Which means we bind the registers with the variables defined in C

460
00:14:16,910 --> 00:14:18,650
We tie them together

461
00:14:18,650 --> 00:14:19,690
made a mapping

462
00:14:19,690 --> 00:14:23,870
telling the compiler how to carry out this mapping process

463
00:14:23,870 --> 00:14:29,110
so it's really a correspondence effect

464
00:14:29,110 --> 00:14:31,550
it's really telling it the corresponding effect

465
00:14:31,550 --> 00:14:34,590
now, once that purpose is achieved

466
00:14:34,590 --> 00:14:37,170
the compiler will, in the actual process

467
00:14:37,170 --> 00:14:40,910
in the actual compilation process, assign us an actual register

468
00:14:40,910 --> 00:14:42,770
and then take this register and these things

469
00:14:42,770 --> 00:14:43,810
and map them together

470
00:14:43,810 --> 00:14:45,070
and of course, on our side here

471
00:14:45,070 --> 00:14:47,650
you can see there's also this kind of R syntax written here

472
00:14:47,650 --> 00:14:48,890
this R syntax

473
00:14:48,890 --> 00:14:50,830
is really just telling it

474
00:14:50,830 --> 00:14:54,550
that is, we want to take this variable A and this variable C

475
00:14:54,550 --> 00:14:56,270
and map them to registers

476
00:14:56,270 --> 00:14:59,790
you can also specify that you put this variable on the stack

477
00:14:59,790 --> 00:15:01,230
and map it to memory

478
00:15:01,230 --> 00:15:01,790
that works too

479
00:15:01,790 --> 00:15:06,350
you indicate that with the character M

480
00:15:06,350 --> 00:15:08,750
but here we'll use R

481
00:15:08,750 --> 00:15:11,150
just tell it to use the corresponding register, and that's all there is to it

482
00:15:11,150 --> 00:15:14,150
So this is one way of writing it.

483
00:15:14,150 --> 00:15:15,190
Sometimes,

484
00:15:15,190 --> 00:15:16,530
you'll also see code that other people wrote,

485
00:15:16,530 --> 00:15:17,650
that is, written in a simpler way.

486
00:15:17,650 --> 00:15:19,570
If you want to keep it simple, you can write it like this.

487
00:15:19,570 --> 00:15:22,170
In fact, the effect is exactly the same.

488
00:15:22,170 --> 00:15:22,830
Now here,

489
00:15:22,830 --> 00:15:25,470
all it amounts to is that we don't define any complicated names,

490
00:15:25,470 --> 00:15:26,310
like sum or anything like that,

491
00:15:26,310 --> 00:15:28,050
or that add1 thing from just now.

492
00:15:28,050 --> 00:15:28,890
See what I mean?

